Compiled shaders of the GLES2 renderer (renderer = gles2 in opencmr2.ini).

ogles2.library finds a compiled program by a hash of the exact text of its two
shaders (<24 hex digits>.v3dp, plus a .v3ds record per shader), first in
PROGDIR:shaders/. build.sh copies this drawer to dist/amiga/shaders/. Without
a binary the library starts LIBS:v3dglslc (19 MB, 16 MB stack, seconds per
shader); without the compiler the link fails and the renderer draws with its
general shader (correct, but several times slower on the GPU).

ANY edit of src/render/gles2/gfx_gles2_shaders.h or gles2_fragment.h (even a
space) changes the hashes: regenerate the whole drawer.

Regenerating (on a PiStorm with v3dglslc installed):
  1. Make an empty cache and point ogles2 at it:
       MakeDir WORK:CMR2/shadercache
       Assign OGLES2Cache: WORK:CMR2/shadercache
     and start the game with Stack 16777216 (WORK:CMR2/gles2.script).
     Delete PROGDIR:gles2_shaders.lst so the list starts empty too.
  2. Play through everything: the menus and movies, a race in each environment
     map mode (none, Standard, Bump, Cubic), sunny, foggy, night and rainy
     stages, the replay and the garage/race setup. The log (opencmr2.log) lists
     every shader as "built in N ms"; the first run says COMPILED.
  3. Copy WORK:CMR2/shadercache/#? here (the .v3dp and .v3ds files; also .v3dw
     and .v3da if any appear) and delete the older ones.
  4. Test without the compiler: rename LIBS:v3dglslc, Avail FLUSH, start the
     game from a drawer that has shaders/ and look for "COMPILED" or "FAILED" in
     the log: there must be none. Then put the compiler back.
